 **Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
-*   **Memory Constraints:** Standard feed-forward methods fail to train at 2K resolution or higher due to memory limits (e.g., NoPoSplat requires >61 GB for 1024x576 primitives) [S1, unofficial]. LGTM trains at 2K/4K with <30 GB [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Holes in Rendering:** Without high-resolution supervision, predicted Gaussians may result in areas with holes as they are not anti-aliased [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Memory Constraints:** Standard feed-forward methods fail to train at 2K resolution or higher due to memory constraints (e.g., NoPoSplat fails at 2K/4K) [S1, unofficial]. LGTM mitigates this by decoupling geometry and appearance [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Holes in Rendering:** Without high-resolution supervision in Stage 1, predicted Gaussians may result in areas with holes as they are not anti-aliased [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Memory:** LGTM trains successfully at 2K and 4K resolutions using under 30 GB of memory [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Scalability:** Scaling from 512 to 4K requires 64x more pixels, but LGTM adds only modest memory and time overhead because it maintains a compact set of geometric primitives and scales per-primitive textures instead [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths:** Enables high-fidelity 4K novel view synthesis without per-scene optimization; decouples geometry and appearance; versatile across monocular, posed two-view, unposed two-view, and multi-view inputs [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Limitations:** Requires a staged training strategy; relies on the accuracy of the underlying baseline method (e.g., Flash3D, DepthSplat, NoPoSplat, VGGT) for initial geometry prediction [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Memory:** LGTM trains successfully at 2K and 4K resolutions using under 30 GB of memory, whereas NoPoSplat fails to train at 2K or higher due to memory constraints (OOM) [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Scalability:** LGTM scales efficiently at inference; a 64x pixel increase adds only modest memory and time overhead [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Performance:** Enables high-fidelity 4K novel view synthesis, a capability previously out of reach for feed-forward methods [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Strengths:** Decouples geometry and appearance, allowing compact primitives to represent rich textures; feed-forward (no per-scene optimization); scalable to 4K.
+*   **Limitations:** Requires a dual-network architecture; relies on high-resolution supervision during training to prevent holes [S1, unofficial].
+*   **When to use:** When high-resolution (4K) novel view synthesis is required without per-scene optimization, and memory is a constraint for standard feed-forward methods.
+*   **When not to use:** When per-scene optimization is acceptable and lower resolutions are sufficient, or when computational resources for training a large feed-forward network are unavailable.
 
 ## Background
-LGTM was published as a conference paper at ICLR 2026 by Yixing Lao, Xuyang Bai, Xiaoyang Wu, Nuoyuan Yan, Zixin Luo, Jean-Daniel Nahmias, Yanghai Tsin, Shiwei Li, and Hengshuang Zhao from HKU and Apple [S1, unofficial]. It addresses the limitations of prior textured Gaussian methods that required per-scene optimization and could not generalize across scenes in a feed-forward manner [S1, unofficial].
